            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In mainstream Christian teaching, 'belief' primarily means personal trust and reliance upon God and Jesus Christ for salvation, which goes beyond mere intellectual assent to doctrinal facts.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Belief means accepting God's testimony as true and authoritative, and consequently acting upon it, meaning that obedience is inherent to belief and persistent disobedience constitutes unbelief.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Personal trust and reliance on Christ (fiducia) as an instrument of salvation.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Receiving God's testimony as governing truth and actively obeying it.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book requires obedient action as a defining boundary of genuine belief, explicitly classifying a refusal to obey as unbelief. The primary consensus defines belief primarily as inward trust (though often producing action), whereas the book merges belief with active obedience.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book rejects the common evangelical framing of belief solely as a passive instrument of 'personal trust' (as noted in its common-view critique), arguing instead that true belief intrinsically involves governing action and obedience.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elevates a recognized alternative view (belief as allegiance/obedience) over the primary consensus view (belief as trust/reliance). It agrees with the consensus in rejecting mere intellectual assent, but refines the boundary by requiring obedience as constitutive of belief rather than merely a byproduct.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Mainstream Protestant theology typically distinguishes sharply between faith (belief/trust) and works (obedience) to preserve the doctrine of justification by faith alone. While recognizing they are inseparable in the life of a believer, defining belief itself as 'acting upon it' blurs the theological distinction between the root (faith) and the fruit (obedience).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
